 The OPACK and framing tests use fixtures from pyatv 0.18.0 and the [published OPACK examples](https://pyatv.dev/documentation/protocols/#opack).
 
+## AirPlay metadata compatibility
+
+DataStream frames can carry several length-prefixed protobufs. The receive path
+delivers all of them in wire order, including across encrypted TCP fragments.
+`parseDataStreamFrame` retains the first message as `protobufData` for compatibility
+and exposes the complete `protobufMessages` array. Malformed lengths fail instead
+of silently truncating packets or preventing parser progress.
+
+The metadata schema includes volume/capability updates, active player selection,
+client/player removal, grouped outputs and cluster identity. `MRPMessage` and
+`MessageType` are public exports for decoded-message consumers and codec tests.
+Untyped acknowledgements have type zero rather than appearing as media commands.
+The metadata fixtures come from locked pyatv encoders using synthetic data only.
+The receive-path regression verifies a fragmented encrypted pair of batches.
+
+Correlated read-only volume queries use `MRPMessage.getVolume(outputDeviceUID)`
+and reply type `MessageType.GetVolumeResult`. Missing result fields remain absent;
+they are not interpreted as zero. Callers must bind a result to the output
+generation that issued the request, since the reply itself has no output ID.
+
+`MRPMessage.setSystemAudioOutputs(outputDeviceUIDs)` encodes a
+`ModifyOutputContextRequestMessage` with the `SharedSystemAudio` context and the
+UIDs in both `settingDevices` and `clusterAwareSettingDevices`. This context selects
+personal outputs such as same-account AirPods; `SharedAudioPresentation` is the
+AirPlay speaker-group context. The device's reply is only an acknowledgement, so
+confirm the route from later reported output devices. The acknowledgement can be
+slow while tvOS takes AirPods over from another device; wait for it with
+`sendMRPMessageAndWait(request, undefined, timeoutMs, { fatalTimeout: false })` so a
+late or missing acknowledgement rejects only that wait instead of closing the
+connection. Other requests keep the default fatal timeout. Context values follow the
+MediaRemote enum recorded in pyatv#2834, and the fixtures come from pyatv 0.18.0.
+
+Consumers must track explicit active player/output selection and invalidate
+feedback on connection loss; these extensions do not provide a complete reducer.
+
+## AirPlay connection lifetime
+
+Each `AirPlayConnection` owns one lifetime. Pass an optional fourth constructor
+argument `{ timeoutMs, logger }` to set the socket/HTTP/write deadline (default
+10 seconds) and replace debug logging. `connect({ signal })` accepts cancellation
+throughout setup and the connected lifetime. Use a new instance after close or
+failure. `scan({ signal })` also cancels its mDNS browsers and deadline.
+
+Closing settles pending connects, writes, HTTP/RTSP and MRP requests, destroys all
+owned sockets, clears heartbeat timers, and emits one close event. Errors also
+reach registered error listeners. No connection or request is retried. Complete
+encrypted frames are consumed separately from partial plaintext HTTP replies;
+all coalesced replies are processed. RTSP replies match CSeq and MRP replies match
+the request identifier. Untyped MRP acknowledgements are supported when no response
+type is required; errors and wrong response types reject instead of accepting an
+unrelated state push. Only one heartbeat can be outstanding.
+
+Regression tests cover fragmented/reordered replies, immediate replies during
+write, failures, concurrent requests, cancellation, and real loopback TCP cleanup.
+Two read-only sessions against one TV also connected, observed metadata, closed,
+and exited naturally without remaining TCP sockets. Longer outage/soak coverage
+and integration into a persistent application controller remain separate work.
+
